Analysis of downstream conditions shall, at a minimum, include the following:
Ten percent downstream analysis (from all ponds and site in general);
• Ten percent downstream analysis (where site area, is less than ten percent of the drainage basin area);
• Identification of all affected structures between lower limit of site and ten percent analysis point;
• Analysis of all affected structures between lower limit of site and ten percent analysis point;
• Prerouting and postrouting analysis;
• Describe in combination with a topographic map, all culverts, obstructions, existing and potential erosion problems, elevations of existing improvements, and existing drainage complaints, between the downstream property line and the ten percent point;
• Analyze downstream watercourses and receiving conveyances to determine 25-year flow channel velocities. If the nonerosive velocity of the stream is exceeded, detention may be required. Provide calculations with cross-section, depth of flow and velocity in channel;
• Analyze existing pipe systems and culverts for compliance with current development regulation design criteria. Culverts should pass QIOO. If existing pipes are not adequate for increased 100-year flow, detention may be required. (DR 8.2.Ic);
• Hydrograph comparisons for the one-, two-, five-, ten- 25- 50- and 100-year storms for both the downstream property line study point and the point where the drainage basin equals ten times the project area;
• A detailed written description of the first 500 feet off site; at least one photograph looking downstream with some object included in the photograph for scale shall be included;
• Predeveloped on-site hydrograph;
• Basin area at study point excluding on-site area hydrograph;
• Postdeveloped on-site hydrograph;
• Since downstream analysis indicates adverse impacts are expected, provide stormwater detention in basin(s) for the one-, two-, five-, ten-, 25-, 50- and 100-year storm event. Route the 100-year storm through the pond so that there is no overflow from the pond;
• Detain the runoff from the one-year storm for 24 hours. Use the SCS runoff equation to size the volume required. Use appropriate equations to size the orifice
• Schedule a meeting to discuss any county comment(s);
• All hydrographs shall be based on a 24-hour storm.
